Reusing HTTP+SSE gateway as daemon transport works for tmux-style attach clients (EventSource / fetch Accept: text/event-stream); daemon runs long-lived ReAct loops independent of clients. Pitfall: Node single-threaded loop blocks on CPU work. session_id lifecycle: generate UUID on first connect or explicit POST /sessions; store Map<sessionId,{agentState,eventHistory,lastId,clients:Set}>; create→active→detached(no clients)→resume or timeout/GC; persist critical state (SQLite/JSON) for daemon restarts. Handoff/resume via buffered replay using Last-Event-ID: on SSE connect read req.headers['last-event-id'], replay buffered events > that id then stream live; per-session ring buffer (cap 1k-10k); emit 'id: N
data: {...}

'; clients auto-reconnect sending Last-Event-ID. Pitfalls: unbounded buffer memory growth, stale sessions, replay ordering, non-idempotent events — use monotonic per-session IDs. Graceful drain on SIGINT/SIGTERM: register handlers early; on signal set draining state (reject new sessions, health→503), finish current agent steps, flush pending SSE, close connections cleanly with a final 'event: shutdown' frame (avoid raw .end() to prevent client reconnect spam), timeout 10-30s then force exit. Pitfalls: SIGKILL uncatchable, lingering timers/connections, health checks not updated. Notes: prefer persistent over ephemeral runtimes; SSE for agent→client streaming paired with HTTP POST for commands (sidecar pattern); limit buffers + session TTL; avoid over-abstraction.
